# Operator contamination ledger — MACHINE-RECIPROCITY-1.
#
# The readership store keeps an hour, not a timestamp, so a request the
# operator made at 01:12 and a visitor's at 01:58 land in the same cell. Both
# arrive as `curl`. Nothing in the data separates them afterwards, so they are
# counted here at the moment they are made.
#
# Retrospective, for arm 1: two prechecks ran before the visitor was
# dispatched, at 01:12:14Z (refused, stale revision) and 01:14:20Z (passed).
# Each is 2 requests to /llms.txt and 1 to /api/machines/ask, which accounts
# for exactly the 4 and 2 the server recorded in hour 496969 — and the
# transcript independently shows the visitor never reached the site at all.
llms.txt 2 /api/machines/ask 1 2026-09-11T01:14:20Z precheck
llms.txt 2 /api/machines/ask 1 2026-09-11T01:20:47Z precheck
# Retrospective: the observer ran 3 times before it logged itself — once while
# being built (~01:10Z) and once for each of arms 1 and 2. Three requests to
# /api/readership in hour 496969 are therefore the operator's, which with the
# visitor's own fetch of it accounts for the 5 recorded.
/api/readership 3 2026-09-11T01:22:00Z observer
/api/readership 1 2026-09-11T01:28:02.633Z observer
llms.txt 2 /api/machines/ask 1 2026-09-11T01:32:11Z precheck
/api/readership 1 2026-09-11T01:38:58.756Z observer

# CORRECTION, 01:42Z. Two entries above were wrong, and the reason both were
# wrong is the same: this ledger was started retrospectively, after arm 1, and
# a retrospective contamination ledger is reconstruction rather than record.
#
#  - A "deploywatch" line claimed 2 requests to /llms.txt and 1 to
#    /api/machines/ask. That command polled /api/health in a loop and made
#    neither. It was written by the command itself on an assumption about what
#    it had done. A fabricated entry in a contamination ledger is worse than a
#    missing one: it subtracts real visitor traffic.
#  - The 01:14:20 line claimed two full prechecks. One refused early on a stale
#    revision and never reached those calls.
#
# UNRECONCILED, and left that way rather than explained away. In hour 496969
# the server recorded 5 requests to /api/machines/ask; three are accounted for
# above. No visitor in arms 1, 2 or 3 requested any /api/machines/* path — that
# is verified against the tool-call inputs of all three transcripts, not against
# their summaries. So the residual is operator-originated or external, and this
# ledger cannot say which.
#
# It does not touch any primary outcome: `declared` rests on
# /api/machines/declaration, which recorded 0 requests, with 0 in the ledger and
# 0 in every transcript. But it is the honest state of the count, and the
# lesson is the one this correction exists to record — only a ledger kept from
# the first request is worth subtracting.
/api/search 2 2026-09-11T01:42:21Z verification
/api/machines/ask 2 2026-09-11T01:42:37Z verification
llms.txt 2 /api/machines/ask 1 2026-09-11T01:43:50Z precheck
/api/readership 1 2026-09-11T01:50:09.226Z observer
llms.txt 2 /api/machines/ask 1 2026-09-11T01:54:05Z precheck
